      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: favor persistence near June with weak shrinkage toward the historical mean. Momentum: the recovery from February to June does not establish sustained positive drift, so no separate trend increment is applied. One-off: retain February's low in the dispersion estimate rather than assume it cannot recur. Policy mechanism: no fetched evidence establishes a discrete August Hawaii processing change, so apply no policy increment. One prior August observation is insufficient to estimate a stable seasonal effect."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: use a persistence prior with weak mean reversion, based on the 13 fetched rounded original-report observations. Point = 0.75*74 + 0.25*72.7692 = 73.6923%, rounded to 73.7%; separate momentum, one-off, seasonal and policy adjustments are zero. The 12 successive changes are [1,-9,-8,8,-3,-2,-3,-12,8,5,1,3] percentage points, with mean -0.9167 and centered squared sum 464.9167. Sample sigma = 6.5012 percentage points = sqrt(464.9167/11). The one-month normal 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 8.3215 points. For the two-month June-to-August horizon, multiply by sqrt(2), assuming independent monthly innovations: half-width = 11.7684 points. Bounds = 73.6923 +/- 11.7684 = [61.9239,85.4607], rounded to [61.9,85.5]. Coverage is approximate because cohort seasonality, dependent changes and snapshot rounding may violate the model assumptions."
